 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T05:05+08:00",
 "answer": "The air quality in Singapore is **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy** due to ongoing smoke haze drifting in from regional forest fires in Kalimantan and Sumatra. \n\nData from Singapore's National Environment Agency (NEA) and real-time air quality monitors show:\n\n* **24-hour PSI (Pollutant Standards Index):** **148 – 203** across the island (putting different regions in the *Unhealthy* to *Very Unhealthy* range, with Central Singapore experiencing the highest readings).\n* **1-hour PM2.5 Concentration:** **73 – 116 µg/m³**, which falls into **Band 2 (Elevated)**.\n* **Real-Time AQI (Central Singapore):** **~174 (Unhealthy)**.\n\n**Health Advisory:** \nBecause pollutant levels are elevated, residents are advised to reduce or avoid prolonged and strenuous outdoor physical exertion. Vulnerable individuals (such as the elderly, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic heart or lung conditions) should minimize outdoor activity, keep windows closed, and use air purifiers indoors if available.",
